[[:db/cas 17592186369790 :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=13$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=13.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=169.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-169I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-169I=[\\begin{aligned}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{aligned}]-169[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-169I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{3}{5}|+|\\frac{4}{5}|=\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$" "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=13$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=13.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=169.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-169I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-169I=\\begin{bmatrix}153 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 160\\end{bmatrix}-169\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-169I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{3}{5}|+|\\frac{4}{5}|=\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369797 :question/problem "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}45 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$" "$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}45 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$"] [:db/cas 17592186369797 :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=9.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-9I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-9I=[\\begin{aligned}45 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{aligned}]-9[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-9I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$" "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=9.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-9I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-9I=\\begin{bmatrix}45 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}-9\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-9I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369804 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$"] [:db/cas 17592186369804 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=18$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{28}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{21}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=18$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 12 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{28}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{21}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369806 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186369807 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186369808 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186369809 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186369810 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186369811 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$"] [:db/cas 17592186369811 :question/worked-solution "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\sigma }_{1}={\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$): Seeking non-zero solutions of $({A}^{T}\\,A-5I)v=0,$ we obtain the unit eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $Σ,$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\sigma }_{1}={\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$): Seeking non-zero solutions of $({A}^{T}\\,A-5I)v=0,$ we obtain the unit eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $Σ,$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369813 :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186369814 :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186369815 :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186369816 :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and  \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and  \n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186369817 :answer/value "$U=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$V=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186369845 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369845 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $4$th row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-12|=12.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $4$th row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-12|=12.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369852 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,d=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369852 :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(15+21) \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|144|=144.\n$$" "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{4+2}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(15+21) \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|144|=144.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369873 :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{aligned}].$" "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186369873 :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=3.\n$$" "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186369901 :question/problem "Let $F$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & a \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $F.$\n- **II.** For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in F,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- **III.** For any $A,B\\in F,$ we have $A+B=B+A.$" "Let $F$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}a & a \\\\ 0 & a\\end{bmatrix},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $F.$\n- **II.** For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in F,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- **III.** For any $A,B\\in F,$ we have $A+B=B+A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186369915 :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ and $B=({b}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha ({a}_{ij}-{b}_{ij})=\\alpha {a}_{ij}-\\alpha {b}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A-B)=\\alpha A-\\alpha B.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta {a}_{ij})=(\\alpha \\beta ){a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta A)=(\\alpha \\beta )A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $P.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\notin P\n$$\n\nbecause $-1+0≯0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ and $B=({b}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha ({a}_{ij}-{b}_{ij})=\\alpha {a}_{ij}-\\alpha {b}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A-B)=\\alpha A-\\alpha B.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta {a}_{ij})=(\\alpha \\beta ){a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta A)=(\\alpha \\beta )A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ from $P.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=(-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\notin P\n$$\n\nbecause $-1+0≯0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186370044 :answer/value "x(t)=5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" "x(t)=5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}"] [:db/cas 17592186370045 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}"] [:db/cas 17592186370046 :answer/value "x(t)=5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}" "x(t)=5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}"] [:db/cas 17592186370047 :answer/value "x(t)=5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" "x(t)=5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}"] [:db/cas 17592186370048 :answer/value "x(t)=5[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}" "x(t)=5\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}"] [:db/cas 17592186370049 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |" "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{2t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186370049 :question/worked-solution "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n3{c}_{1}=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3+{c}_{2}=1\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\\,+\\,(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\\,+\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=1 \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n3{c}_{1}=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3+{c}_{2}=1\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\,+\\,(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\,+\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186370065 :question/problem "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$*" "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186370065 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3/2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2}=14\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{2}=-14$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-2(-14)=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=37.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =37[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}-14[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3/2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-3{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ {c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2}=14\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{2}=-14$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-2(-14)=9\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=37.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =37\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}-14\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186370067 :answer/value "x(t)=37[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}-14[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "x(t)=37\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}-14\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186370068 :answer/value "x(t)=-6[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}-9[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "x(t)=-6\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}-9\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186370069 :answer/value "x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+6[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+6\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186370070 :answer/value "x(t)=16[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+7[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "x(t)=16\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+7\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186370071 :answer/value "x(t)=6[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}-7[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" "x(t)=6\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}-7\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186370129 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm \\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$ |  |  |" "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\pm \\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$ |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186370129 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & -{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot \\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & -{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\text{i}t) & ={e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}\\cdot \\text{i}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186370138 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $1,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $1,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186370138 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3{e}^{t}t, \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\nt{e}^{t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(1-t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3{e}^{t}t, \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{t}t+{e}^{t}(1-t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186370149 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $3(t-1){e}^{2(t-1)}$ |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $0$ |  |  |  |  |" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $3(t-1){e}^{2(t-1)}$ |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $0$ |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186370149 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{2(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3(t-1){e}^{2(t-1)} \\\\ 3{e}^{2(t-1)}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3(t-1){e}^{2(t-1)} \\\\ 3{e}^{2(t-1)}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{2(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-1)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3(t-1){e}^{2(t-1)} \\\\ 3{e}^{2(t-1)}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2s} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3(t-1){e}^{2(t-1)} \\\\ 3{e}^{2(t-1)}\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186370201 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4x-2xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2xy)=4-2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2xy)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+5xy)=5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+5xy)=-3+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}4-2y & -2x \\\\ 5y & -3+5x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}4-2(0) & -2(0) \\\\ 5(0) & -3+5(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4x-2xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2xy)=4-2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2xy)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+5xy)=5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+5xy)=-3+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}4-2y & -2x \\\\ 5y & -3+5x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}4-2(0) & -2(0) \\\\ 5(0) & -3+5(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186370240 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0.7x-0.35xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-0.4y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0.7x-0.35xy)=0.7-0.35y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0.7x-0.35xy)=-0.35x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.4y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.4y+2xy)=-0.4+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}0.7-0.35y & -0.35x \\\\ 2y & -0.4+2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}0.7-0.35(2) & -0.35(0.2) \\\\ 2(2) & -0.4+2(0.2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -0.07 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -0.07 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.07) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.28 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 0.2\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is $\\text{a stable}$ $\\text{center}.$\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)." "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0.7x-0.35xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-0.4y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0.7x-0.35xy)=0.7-0.35y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0.7x-0.35xy)=-0.35x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.4y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.4y+2xy)=-0.4+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7-0.35y & -0.35x \\\\ 2y & -0.4+2x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}0.7-0.35(2) & -0.35(0.2) \\\\ 2(2) & -0.4+2(0.2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -0.07 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & -0.07 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.07) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.28 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 0.2\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is $\\text{a stable}$ $\\text{center}.$\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)."] [:db/cas 17592186370312 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186370312 :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186370321 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -12 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -12 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186370321 :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -12 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) & -12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) & -12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -12 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) & -12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) & -12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186370423 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=3x(1-0.3x)-4xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-y+xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x(1-0.3x)-4xy)=3-1.8x-4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x(1-0.3x)-4xy)=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+xy)=-1+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}3-1.8x-4y & -4x \\\\ y & -1+x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}3-1.8(0)-4(0) & -4(0) \\\\ 0 & -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=3x(1-0.3x)-4xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-y+xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x(1-0.3x)-4xy)=3-1.8x-4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x(1-0.3x)-4xy)=-4x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+xy)=-1+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}3-1.8x-4y & -4x \\\\ y & -1+x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}3-1.8(0)-4(0) & -4(0) \\\\ 0 & -1+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186370462 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4x(1-5x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-5y+10xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-5x)-xy)=4-40x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-5x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y+10xy)=10y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y+10xy)=-5+10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}4-40x-y & -x \\\\ 10y & -5+10x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}4-40(0.2)-0 & -0.2 \\\\ 10(0) & -5+10(0.2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -0.2 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$" "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4x(1-5x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-5y+10xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-5x)-xy)=4-40x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-5x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y+10xy)=10y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y+10xy)=-5+10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}4-40x-y & -x \\\\ 10y & -5+10x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}4-40(0.2)-0 & -0.2 \\\\ 10(0) & -5+10(0.2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -0.2 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186370501 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-2x)-xy)=4-16x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+2xy)=-3+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}4-16x-y & -x \\\\ 2y & -3+2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}4-16(0.5)-0 & -0.5 \\\\ 2(0) & -3+2(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -0.5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$" "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=4x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-2x)-xy)=4-16x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+2xy)=-3+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}4-16x-y & -x \\\\ 2y & -3+2x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}4-16(0.5)-0 & -0.5 \\\\ 2(0) & -3+2(0.5)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -0.5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{sink}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186370540 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(1-x)-xy)=1-2x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+2xy)=-1+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}1-2x-y & -x \\\\ 2y & -1+2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}1-2(0.5)-0.5 & -0.5 \\\\ 2(0.5) & -1+2(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & -0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-0.5-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-0.5-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-1(-0.5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (0.5+\\lambda )+0.5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.5\\lambda +0.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.25\\pm 0.25\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$" "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(1-x)-xy)=1-2x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+2xy)=-1+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}1-2x-y & -x \\\\ 2y & -1+2x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0.5\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}1-2(0.5)-0.5 & -0.5 \\\\ 2(0.5) & -1+2(0.5)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5 & -0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-0.5-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 1 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-0.5-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-1(-0.5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (0.5+\\lambda )+0.5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.5\\lambda +0.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.25\\pm 0.25\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186370654 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"] [:db/cas 17592186370654 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ 