} & {v}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|?$"] [:db/cas 17592186371283 :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-16I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-16I=[\\begin{aligned}68 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 29\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}52 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$" "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the second column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=16.$ Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-16I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-16I=\\begin{bmatrix}68 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 29\\end{bmatrix}-16\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}52 & 26 \\\\ 26 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{12}|+|{v}_{22}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371290 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$"] [:db/cas 17592186371290 :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=100.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}58 & 42 \\\\ 42 & 58\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-100I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-100I=[\\begin{aligned}58 & 42 \\\\ 42 & 58\\end{aligned}]-100[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-42 & 42 \\\\ 42 & -42\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-100I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=100.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}58 & 42 \\\\ 42 & 58\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-100I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-100I=\\begin{bmatrix}58 & 42 \\\\ 42 & 58\\end{bmatrix}-100\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-42 & 42 \\\\ 42 & -42\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-100I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371297 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$"] [:db/cas 17592186371297 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371299 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371300 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371301 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371302 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371303 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371304 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$"] [:db/cas 17592186371304 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{10} \\\\ \\sqrt{10}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371306 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371307 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371308 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371309 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371310 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371325 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$"] [:db/cas 17592186371325 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371327 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371328 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371329 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371330 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371331 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371332 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{11} & {v}_{12} \\\\ {v}_{21} & {v}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$"] [:db/cas 17592186371332 :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=49.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}41 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-49I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-49I=[\\begin{aligned}41 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 17\\end{aligned}]-49[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -16 \\\\ -16 & -32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-49I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$" "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=49.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}41 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-49I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-49I=\\begin{bmatrix}41 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 17\\end{bmatrix}-49\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -16 \\\\ -16 & -32\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-49I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371339 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $Σ,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the first column of $U?$"] [:db/cas 17592186371339 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${v}_{i}$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=1.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bma