1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{30}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 90\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find 