onding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{6}^{-1} & \\,\\,\\,0 & 0 \\\\ \\,\\,0 & \\,(-9)^{-1} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For an \"almost\" zero $m\\times n$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the \"almost\" zero $n\\times m$ matrix with entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}^{-1},\\,{\\sigma }_{2}^{-1},\\,…,\\,{\\sigma }_{r}^{-1}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal.\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{+} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{6}^{-1} & \\,\\,\\,0 & 0 \\\\ \\,\\,0 & \\,(-9)^{-1} & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371778 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371779 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371780 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371781 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371782 :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "-\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186371783 :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186371783 :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\e