{′}(t)=$" "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ {e}^{u}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=4t-3,$ then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186373118 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{u})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ {e}^{u}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u(t)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u(t)) \\\\ {e}^{u(t)}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ {e}^{4t-3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ 4{e}^{4t-3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({e}^{u})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\ {e}^{u}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-3)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u(t)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u(t)) \\\\ {e}^{u(t)}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ {e}^{4t-3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ 4{e}^{4t-3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373120 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ 4{e}^{4t-3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ 4{e}^{4t-3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186373121 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186373122 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ 4{e}^{4t-3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ 4{e}^{4t-3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186373123 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ (4t-3){e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186373124 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ {e}^{4t-3}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t-3) \\\\ {e}^{4t-3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186373278 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ -16 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$"] [:db/cas 17592186373278 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-2)+(-16)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-3)+(-16)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-3)}{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-2)+(-16)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4}{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{(-18)\\cdot (-3)+(-16)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-3)}{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}w=\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373280 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186373281 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186373282 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186373283 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186373284 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186373285 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$" "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$"] [:db/cas 17592186373285 :question/worked-solution "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{15\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 6+(-3)\\cdot (-12)}{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-12)^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{15\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=4[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set ${v,w}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1}}$ and $\\text{Span}{{a}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{15\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 6+(-3)\\cdot (-12)}{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-12)^{2}}v=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{15\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}w=4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373287 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]." "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}."] [:db/cas 17592186373288 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]." "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}."] [:db/cas 17592186373289 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -13\\end{aligned}]." "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}."] [:db/cas 17592186373290 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]." "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}."] [:db/cas 17592186373291 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]." "\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}."] [:db/cas 17592186373298 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x-y \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v-1$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v-1=3\\,⟹\\,v=4-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v-1$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v-1=2\\,⟹\\,v=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=4-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,3).$\n\n![](images/4d/4d719cc14094ad632069bcdb9307929bae3030072c1ce3d0f57f3ca682e0aac0.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,3\\le v\\le 4-{u}^{2}}.\n$$" "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x-y \\\\ y+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v-1$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+y=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}+v-1=3\\,⟹\\,v=4-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v-1$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\,⟹\\,v-1=2\\,⟹\\,v=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=4-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,3).$\n\n![](images/4d/4d719cc14094ad632069bcdb9307929bae3030072c1ce3d0f57f3ca682e0aac0.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,3\\le v\\le 4-{u}^{2}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373311 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y+1\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v+1$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+y=2\\,⟹\\,(y+1)=3-(x-y)^{2}\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhile substituting $y=v-1$ into the equation of the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,1\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}.}.\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9cec7ff29557aa14cd19309e9180482a44091e7ef215205285822674e7bc25b0.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(3-{u}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=y+1\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $y=v+1$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+y=2\\,⟹\\,(y+1)=3-(x-y)^{2}\\,⟹\\,v=3-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhile substituting $y=v-1$ into the equation of the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}v=3-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm \\sqrt{2} \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,1\\le v\\le 3-{u}^{2}.}.\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9cec7ff29557aa14cd19309e9180482a44091e7ef215205285822674e7bc25b0.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}[v]_{1}^{3-{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(3-{u}^{2}-1)\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}}(2-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[2u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-\\sqrt{2}}^{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =(2\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})-(-2\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373318 :question/problem "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal." "Consider the vector $x$ and the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFind the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ given that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal."] [:db/cas 17592186373318 :question/worked-solution "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\overset{^}{x}) & =‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-4)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(5-4)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}=\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{a}_{1}=2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}=\\frac{3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\hat{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-4)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(5-4)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373325 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-y,\\,-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4-y=0 \\\\ -x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,4).$ Since the point $(0,4)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(0) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,4-{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=0,$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(0,4).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,4),\\,(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $8$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $8$ at the points $(2,0).$" "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4-y,\\,-x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4-y=0 \\\\ -x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,4).$ Since the point $(0,4)$ does not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(0) \\\\  & =4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,4-{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4(t)-(t)(4-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=0,$ inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain the candidate point, $(0,4).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain three candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,4),\\,(2,0),\\,(-2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $8$ | $-8$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $8$ at the points $(2,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186373332 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-2y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-2y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(x-2y)^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,v-{u}^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}+2 \\\\ v=3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,3).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{2}+2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca755cd11609e7c704adb37eb867ff8cc48bc5fc44b29cd777269be5791d9160.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}+2}^{3}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[v]_{{u}^{2}+2}^{3}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3-({u}^{2}+2))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-2y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x-2y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(x-2y)^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,v-{u}^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}+2 \\\\ v=3\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 1 \\\\ v=3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 1,3).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{2}+2\\le v\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/ca/ca755cd11609e7c704adb37eb867ff8cc48bc5fc44b29cd777269be5791d9160.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}+2}^{3}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[v]_{{u}^{2}+2}^{3}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3-({u}^{2}+2))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373339 :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+3x,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+3x=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3x)^{2}-x=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=3\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-3 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 2 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,{u}^{2}-3\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/26/2687f0cc14eb0b476ea5f4ba7272e7f248bb7dcb276a0e2b0c39e551501b011d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(-1)^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[v]_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(1-({u}^{2}-3))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[4u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+3x,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+3x=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3x)^{2}-x=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=3\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-3 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 2 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,{u}^{2}-3\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/26/2687f0cc14eb0b476ea5f4ba7272e7f248bb7dcb276a0e2b0c39e551501b011d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(-1)^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[v]_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(1-({u}^{2}-3))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[4u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373360 :question/problem "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" "What is the area scale factor of the linear transformation $T$ that is given by its standard matrix \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373367 :question/problem "A triangle $D$ has vertices $(-3,0),(1,0),(0,4).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "A triangle $D$ has vertices $(-3,0),(1,0),(0,4).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373374 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x-y \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $x+y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+x+y=0\\,⟹\\,(x-y)^{2}+(x+y)=0\\,⟹\\,v=-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=u \\\\ x+y=v\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{v-u}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the equation of the given line yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{v-u}{2}=0 & \\,⟹\\,v=u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to find the intersections of the resulting lines in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v=-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=u\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u(u+1)=0 \\\\ v=u.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(-1,-1).$ \n\n![](images/05/05d7e65dca3c6d116d01be9e57fc83f3e3edcda4135a3f96fea82af8db4f2d1e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 0,\\,u\\le v\\le -{u}^{2}\\}.\n$$" "First, notice that we are given that \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x-y \\\\ x+y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x-y=u$ and $x+y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2}+x+y=0\\,⟹\\,(x-y)^{2}+(x+y)=0\\,⟹\\,v=-{u}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y=u \\\\ x+y=v\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{v-u}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the equation of the given line yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{v-u}{2}=0 & \\,⟹\\,v=u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to find the intersections of the resulting lines in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v=-{u}^{2} \\\\ v=u\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u(u+1)=0 \\\\ v=u.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(-1,-1).$ \n\n![](images/05/05d7e65dca3c6d116d01be9e57fc83f3e3edcda4135a3f96fea82af8db4f2d1e.png)\n\nAs a result, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}(D)=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 0,\\,u\\le v\\le -{u}^{2}\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373381 :question/problem "A triangle $D$ has vertices $(0,0),(-1,0),(0,-1).$  What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "A triangle $D$ has vertices $(0,0),(-1,0),(0,-1).$  What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373449 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $-2{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $-2{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373449 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x\\cdot {q}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{2}=-2\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x\\cdot {q}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{2}=-2\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373456 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373456 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & =x\\cdot {q}_{3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{3},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & =x\\cdot {q}_{3} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373463 :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $5{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $5{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{3}$ is the third component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373463 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{3},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{3}}{{q}_{3}\\cdot {q}_{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 2+(-7)\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-7+0}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5{x}_{3}=5\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}=3.\n$$" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{3},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{3}}{{q}_{3}\\cdot {q}_{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 2+(-7)\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 0}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-7+0}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5{x}_{3}=5\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}=3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373764 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$6$** | **$11$** | **$8$** | **$20$** | **$24$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multi linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$6$** | **$11$** | **$8$** | **$20$** | **$24$** |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $9$ | $16$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multi linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?"] [:db/cas 17592186373764 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --