egin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(\\pi )) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{1}(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377024 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377100 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$"] [:db/cas 17592186377100 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=1 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =1, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-2+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-2+2(1) \\\\  & =0, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =2+{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2+(1)-(0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=1 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-2 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =1, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-2+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-2+2(1) \\\\  & =0, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =2+{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =2+(1)-(0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186377102 :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377103 :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" "y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377104 :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" "y=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377105 :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" "y=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377106 :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" "y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377107 :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186377107 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-1.$" "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186377114 :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}?$" "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{L}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186377114 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=3 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=3$ and ${y}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=3 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=3$ and ${y}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}=5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186377121 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 22\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$"] [:db/cas 17592186377121 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-6 \\\\ -7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-6$ and ${y}_{2}=-20.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}].$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 22\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-6 \\\\ -7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-6$ and ${y}_{2}=-20.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186377123 :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -22\\end{aligned}]" "y=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -22\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377124 :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}]" "y=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377125 :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" "y=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377126 :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" "y=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377127 :answer/value "y=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" "y=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377137 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$ {{field-1}} $\\cdot \\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-1$ |  |  |" "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$ {{field-1}} $\\cdot \\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-1$ |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186377137 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-5{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -5[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-5a \\\\ -5b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2a+b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-5a \\\\ -5b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2a+b+8 \\\\ 2b+7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a+b+8=-5a \\\\ 2b+7=-5b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n2b+7=-5b\\,⇒\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n2a+(-1)+8=-5a\\,⇒\\,a=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-5{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ -5\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-5a \\\\ -5b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a+b \\\\ 2b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-5a \\\\ -5b\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a+b+8 \\\\ 2b+7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a+b+8=-5a \\\\ 2b+7=-5b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n2b+7=-5b\\,⇒\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n2a+(-1)+8=-5a\\,⇒\\,a=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377142 :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 12 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-3t-12 \\\\ -8t-8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | $-25$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $16$ |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |" "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 12 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}-3t-12 \\\\ -8t-8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | $-25$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $16$ |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186377142 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3t-12 \\\\ -8t-8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 12 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-3t-12 \\\\ -8t-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+12({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\ -8({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+11({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3t-12 \\\\ -8t-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(-9{a}_{1}+12{a}_{2}-3)t+(-9{b}_{1}+12{b}_{2}-12) \\\\ (-8{a}_{1}+11{a}_{2}-8)t+(-8{b}_{1}+11{b}_{2}-8)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-9{a}_{1}+12{a}_{2}-3=0 \\\\ -8{a}_{1}+11{a}_{2}-8=0 \\\\ -9{b}_{1}+12{b}_{2}-12={a}_{1} \\\\ -8{b}_{1}+11{b}_{2}-8={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first two equations, we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=21,\\,{a}_{2}=16.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-25,\\,{b}_{2}=-16.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-25 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3t-12 \\\\ -8t-8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 12 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}-3t-12 \\\\ -8t-8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-9({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+12({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\ -8({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+11({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3t-12 \\\\ -8t-8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-9{a}_{1}+12{a}_{2}-3)t+(-9{b}_{1}+12{b}_{2}-12) \\\\ (-8{a}_{1}+11{a}_{2}-8)t+(-8{b}_{1}+11{b}_{2}-8)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-9{a}_{1}+12{a}_{2}-3=0 \\\\ -8{a}_{1}+11{a}_{2}-8=0 \\\\ -9{b}_{1}+12{b}_{2}-12={a}_{1} \\\\ -8{b}_{1}+11{b}_{2}-8={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first two equations, we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=21,\\,{a}_{2}=16.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-25,\\,{b}_{2}=-16.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}21 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}-25 \\\\ -16\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377575 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-kx+3y=3$ and $x+2y=0$ intersect at one point, then\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0$, which implies that ${A}^{-1}$ exists ($A$ is non-singular). Additionally, the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $x=⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-kx+3y=3$ and $x+2y=0$ intersect at one point, then\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0$, which implies that ${A}^{-1}$ exists ($A$ is non-singular). Additionally, the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $x=⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines."] [:db/cas 17592186377577 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution"] [:db/cas 17592186377578 :answer/value "${[\\begin{aligned}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}$ exists" "${\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}$ exists"] [:db/cas 17592186377579 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution"] [:db/cas 17592186377580 :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=0" "det\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=0"] [:db/cas 17592186377581 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is singular" "$\\begin{bmatrix}-k & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular"] [:db/cas 17592186377668 :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is **not** invertible\n- **II.** The lines $2x-3y=1$ and $2x-2y=2$ intersect at exactly one point\n- **III.** $Ax=0$ has no solution" "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is **not** invertible\n- **II.** The lines $2x-3y=1$ and $2x-2y=2$ intersect at exactly one point\n- **III.** $Ax=0$ has no solution"] [:db/cas 17592186377668 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false since, according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false since, according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [:db/cas 17592186377735 :question/problem "If $U=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]$, then ${U}^{3}=$" "If $U=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$, then ${U}^{3}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186377735 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${U}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{2} & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${U}^{2}=U,$ so $U$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${U}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{3} & ={U}^{2}\\cdot U \\\\  & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & ={U}^{2} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{U}^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "First, we compute ${U}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{2} & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${U}^{2}=U,$ so $U$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${U}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{U}^{3} & ={U}^{2}\\cdot U \\\\  & =U\\cdot U \\\\  & ={U}^{2} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{U}^{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377737 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377738 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377739 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377740 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377741 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186377756 :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.$" "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186377756 :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"]]
